#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/id #uuid "1900f9ee-5295-5e56-b9d5-2dda8649b212" :question/math-academy-id "e-9684" :question/problem "![](images/71/7178cf6a642786f249a22cab09178eaa62e63e47a4e3bb28969ca90f4d97db95.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the distances some athletes in a sports club cover during a training session, and the time (in minutes) it takes. Use the trend line to estimate how long it takes a club athlete to run $10$ km." :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=10$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/c5/c595c42406bbf7ad4d71b6ceb9914b839f529b26135c467231a38b3cc48223c8.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/28/28f757380ba22cddc96fbd382578a6b8dc9bba258adfec704413047e73d54c93.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/85/85754fb62bbee8c597a71ad8fe30e5eabca3ed9c6304b1832e14788b1e809bcd.png)\n\nTherefore, a club athlete can run $10$ kilometers in approximately $45$ minutes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/id #uuid "2bc871c4-b557-5cff-8ab2-55d9cb6e24fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9690" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ecea6126e2bc887610e5a775f6cc82906c4eb1f569277bbc626d0502bd9efa.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of an artwork, in thousands of dollars, several decades after it was created. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the work of art to be worth $\\[math]5\\,000.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1.5)$ and $(4,3).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/76/761a2d3608402bb18b3574040556618d5bb0fa2c9bb53ddcb382f3779ae1af0b.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $(x_1,y_1) = (1,1.5)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (4,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny = mx + b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1.5}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{3} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m = 0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 3 & =0.5(4)+b \\\\ 3 & =2+b \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + 1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = 5$ into the equation above and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+1 \\\\ 5 & =0.5x+1 \\\\ 0.5x & =4 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the artwork will be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$ approximately $8$ decades after it was created.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=5$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/id #uuid "3a864a65-e753-5bd5-b4fd-b3e95fc5a2de" :question/math-academy-id "e-9692" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecce4e60594a69e040328cc8f65300f7ad1ecfe20806d9be08f154fe2beb1246.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between a book’s spine thickness (in millimetres) and the total number of pages for a random sample of children's books. The equation of the trend line is $y = 10x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the thickness of a book with $200$ pages." :question/worked-solution "To predict the thickness in a book with $200$ pages, we need to substitute $y = 200$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10x \\\\ 200 & =10x \\\\ \\frac{200}{10} & =x \\\\ 20 & =x \\\\ x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, a book with $200$ pages should have approximately a thickness of $20\\text{ mm}.$\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=200$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f440f8d-212e-598d-8f9f-659b6451b176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266851} {:answer/id #uuid "52760bde-f5a5-5528-95ef-7bbe23fe19c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266881} {:answer/id #uuid "584b3843-6687-57b7-ac8c-269a9c085fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266932} {:answer/id #uuid "78ec6a8d-4d13-5508-b86f-b1f23b549353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267209} {:answer/id #uuid "9d86b59d-0202-5aff-b73f-257b8701b1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52760bde-f5a5-5528-95ef-7bbe23fe19c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266881} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ecb75f6-2653-5a85-866a-87ded5f8c3cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9a2fa13-4227-5518-b473-4e39aa559784" :question/math-academy-id "q-133774" :question/problem "![](images/47/47b77320474a559f69e6ef08b8f6638aac920b05014820ab1b4b82690059c997.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=-0.5x+9.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $x$ when $y=2.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We can estimate the required value by substituting $y=2$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2 & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2+0.5x & =9 \\\\ 0.5x & =7 \\\\ x & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=2$ we have $x≈14.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=2$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aab51f6-755e-5c2b-a979-93b3e59cb2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266240} {:answer/id #uuid "8041a29d-50f5-54d4-a6ed-a212dcad5925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267274} {:answer/id #uuid "88b300ed-284b-523e-a37a-3ebe7a03844f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267358} {:answer/id #uuid "9954fa5f-2988-531a-8e47-7027c3b0cfe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267528} {:answer/id #uuid "e66a6c54-b2a4-5190-88ea-073e05e84fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8041a29d-50f5-54d4-a6ed-a212dcad5925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267274} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d1427ab-f364-5e07-8dba-f42d822eb655" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f98bb3a-554c-5cbd-87ea-206e88b640d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-133785" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9fed391b00d0159e58b2e760e0c68c5b0be656a440ae898a932bfec64469b5bb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020.$\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the year in which the population will be approximately $40\\,000.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,56)$ and $(18,48).$*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/5b/5b2ab039994e7c2306cad7e30717fb136b5dfc3a028db284a0e95f49b56d580f.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,56)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(18,48).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{48-56}{18-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{16} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,56):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 56 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 56 & =-1+b \\\\ 57 & =b \\\\ b & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+57.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=40$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 40 & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 0.5x+40 & =57 \\\\ 0.5x & =17 \\\\ x & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the population will be $40\\,000$ around the year $2000+34=2034.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=40$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f64d86-98ef-5369-b4fb-15c220217b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549424} {:answer/id #uuid "41b26ab6-dcc5-53e7-b4be-5368fc7fcf38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549451} {:answer/id #uuid "4837a9be-da87-5c2a-937a-176ee878175a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549465} {:answer/id #uuid "558b121a-2eef-5148-999a-63dbe7a14557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549489} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e7ccce-2e82-5e1e-8aec-fe59da6cffeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4837a9be-da87-5c2a-937a-176ee878175a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549465} :answer-field/id #uuid "657377ca-bf35-54d0-beff-26dc9f7a2c08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1131d502-6491-586e-9e5c-4aa6c2f2c8de" :question/math-academy-id "q-133851" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb46d101101d3d2bdb4afcfcc1436f1a62137389db2ffc3fde0cff71054e7f1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=27-0.5x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $y$ when $x=16.$" :question/worked-solution "We can estimate the required value by substituting $x=16$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =27-0.5x \\\\  & =27-0.5(16) \\\\  & =27-8 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=16,$ we have $y≈19.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=16$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b2aac3-d842-5603-b058-bfefeaaf8523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266355} {:answer/id #uuid "32c3e432-a7bb-5021-8586-2138170f15b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266591} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab4274a-1a29-5e99-8723-236f67e45ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266663} {:answer/id #uuid "46fec5f3-e275-51c9-b5c7-5675fa16b87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266776} {:answer/id #uuid "b53b984b-1eac-5396-a243-f30a552dad06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab4274a-1a29-5e99-8723-236f67e45ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266663} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3b9d32-3619-56be-b588-21f8903d05d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd69890e-422d-56db-a361-c19cc01c3d0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133880" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4de50fc0b2b2f05b9ca60e28ffc65900a2b0780549fbf1cf32150a903a08faf5.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $x$ when $y=20.$" :question/worked-solution "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $y=20$ on the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/b6/b614a0ed708e9b0e333c05daf6adde3f4636daebf00f622fc570ade99d8237d8.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/d6/d6772acc92fc6b1ed7c9efbeb4a292ec21f5d2b8069bd0fa4ad7a8d5f7d79470.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/39/3916c8c4a6bfa6cb558cfa91e268e66d011659753ca23da0396cf2ed9f8d1a8f.png)\n\nTherefore, when $y=20,$ we obtain $x≈7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f4c61a-c56d-54cd-9ef2-fd5516f7e35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549349} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1a6631-58bb-5134-9a94-217bbe53ee56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549371} {:answer/id #uuid "3da7d80f-588f-5952-bfcd-0334bb39a7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549441} {:answer/id #uuid "43b4d286-1020-595c-82a9-fd0db5f180b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549455} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e1124f-fabb-5356-8775-645e555a22b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6e1124f-fabb-5356-8775-645e555a22b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549656} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ef87bd-bbc6-5f5c-a6b4-1dd96adfb505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f611e328-34e9-5ebd-abcf-dae6da9bf9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-133882" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6eb063fffcfd0464cbd8cf79811d9ed2ae1e820a30ec621f50e0cc3b9e150e35.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=5-0.25x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $y$ when $x=10.$" :question/worked-solution "We can estimate the required value by substituting $x=10$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-0.25x \\\\  & =5-0.25(10) \\\\  & =5-2.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=10,$ we have $y≈2.5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ea3c33a-403d-567e-a88d-49804b37e946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549443} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5b549b-f313-5f3a-8a10-a2f5214f2bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549474} {:answer/id #uuid "623e74da-8a64-5b5e-97fd-8808df31847a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549509} {:answer/id #uuid "82709572-bf25-533e-94a9-6be94d2c36f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549560} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b8e27d-8509-52d0-9487-b3bb9fd51889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f5b549b-f313-5f3a-8a10-a2f5214f2bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549474} :answer-field/id #uuid "2afd5bb4-fefc-5765-8bdb-316b07bd305a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b530d38-bb33-510e-b9cb-65e2c39387f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-133883" :question/problem "![](images/df/dff61f08e09f21935f616788e54dfb56e0d0a89f38cce9fbc6dc7333d21ba5f1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $x$ when $y=80.$" :question/worked-solution "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $y=80$ on the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/e5/e5923b6c9f0ac4dcf2aee35e67877c425b44f81830bb9f0865c5e21acf81e3f4.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/61/613849314df8014335f7493f9b6b64cb4f2ecd1e585593416f416f1854e12ffa.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/45/4523e742266e3f2d2e759c6e65eb2a7f2bd31e8f4b8473b8e6bfbabec067c9d9.png)\n\nTherefore, when $y=80,$ we obtain $x≈15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0e2ef6-a4a7-52e9-89e2-2a6e9170516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266393} {:answer/id #uuid "59a6b0fc-3ba8-5059-a5c1-cb52b2ade5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266942} {:answer/id #uuid "7588ad3c-568f-5349-9a77-4a085af2b2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267181} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a664eb-70e4-5c96-9f95-22335e300548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267941} {:answer/id #uuid "e15b3e95-70a8-5711-a6bf-c1409251752e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d0e2ef6-a4a7-52e9-89e2-2a6e9170516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266393} :answer-field/id #uuid "30b27f9a-b30c-5df3-8a21-f258f2b0ef8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a78cfca9-9001-58c9-bbd8-9235b77891ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-133884" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e00248d1dd5cb96a5e0099acf5da41913a805dbc388715f0b542aa821a95efa5.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the relationship between the number of hours $x$ a group of people spend watching TV each night and the time $y$ they sleep each night. The equation of the trend line is $y=10-1.25x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time a person sleeps per night if they watch TV for $6$ hours." :question/worked-solution "To predict the number of hours a person sleeps after watching TV for $6$ hours, we need to substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10-1.25x \\\\  & =10-1.25(6) \\\\  & =10-7.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, a person watching TV for $6$ hours sleeps for approximately $2.5$ hours.\n\nNote: The point where $x=6$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121e378e-227c-58b4-9178-bd289e4d1481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549345} {:answer/id #uuid "2e65ceff-fed4-5dfb-bf7c-85e0da5bc187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549416} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee1dd56-d310-5198-9ca6-fff22149c547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549417} {:answer/id #uuid "859eb061-c189-52a9-8d20-8abc934723c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549566} {:answer/id #uuid "f300a523-7514-5583-b6cf-ac152d439a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859eb061-c189-52a9-8d20-8abc934723c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549566} :answer-field/id #uuid "13689481-942b-557a-8a56-b6a42e7e64e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44c1c5d8-2db1-5cbc-a653-89f6c7b7e591" :question/math-academy-id "q-133920" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5d153746d837a857918028aceb623a5c2b9267f00b6baaceb2f24a8172587ee.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $y$ when $x=70.$" :question/worked-solution "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x=70$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/ee/ee670899ebf962939a77959c58219502907681d8cf84329f402b67f6f09a24ba.png)\n\n* Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/f6/f6d34d0c6b95b4b3d5ecf720346ca1e0008ac86c42e8024a2435e2bf792e5031.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/02/0217edcb171859f1aec207d5c248c483a3147dbe74aed277f1313009dbc317c9.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x=70,$ we obtain $y≈5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c938d8-1dfb-5d3f-8853-61890c9bf0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549396} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5d5d01-3161-5cd8-9b54-4a4076db201c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549418} {:answer/id #uuid "54bf6e94-edcd-5556-960e-3ae854a3d5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549488} {:answer/id #uuid "a2006beb-9a32-58ef-bcfb-45b833c60509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549622} {:answer/id #uuid "bd19dc87-8558-5ba0-9185-3db58ee92bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd19dc87-8558-5ba0-9185-3db58ee92bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549671} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc8d1ae-cf76-5d8c-94ef-a0c5ef612072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bba2235-837e-5ee6-9f31-af7dd59da4b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133932" :question/problem "![](images/31/31932ff71e679d32ba3abee0276f7a039e5f204649e63feffeb13029eabc5b88.png)\n\nAn ice cream shop wants to determine the relationship between the outside temperature $x$ (in degrees Celsius) and the number of customers $y$ they serve. The scatter plot above shows the information they collected over several days. The equation of the trend line is $y=2.25x-19.$\n\nEstimate the outside temperature if they served $53$ customers." :question/worked-solution "To predict the outside temperature knowing that they served $53$ customers, we need to substitute $y=53$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.25x-19 \\\\ 53 & =2.25x-19 \\\\ 2.25x & =72 \\\\ x & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, the outside temperature is approximately ${32}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=53$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3177c2c7-aa42-566a-8362-fecceb1e8163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549422} {:answer/id #uuid "528ae067-ae5a-58ad-a807-4a32664e9dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549480} {:answer/id #uuid "5a121c0e-eee5-5094-93d2-ed8591f7049e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549496} {:answer/id #uuid "5f18bd76-f921-5ef0-ab74-e55531698dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549504} {:answer/id #uuid "f96d4a56-8233-583c-bdd1-d33a53b4fcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f18bd76-f921-5ef0-ab74-e55531698dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549504} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a665d06-44a7-566e-9ba1-44800e618b3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf3f9846-0d01-5263-932f-08763c5ce37a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133941" :question/problem "![](images/21/2130538a141293fda6b1bf07c1f0e8e5f7c347a2c688074689b1c13583a11bd2.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $y$ when $x=20.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,23)$ and $(9,27).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/a0/a00942c18663c8e9ea2dd2c5354bbe177f7f8b35a4568c5234b9c92b844f196a.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,23)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(9,27).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27-23}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,23):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5(1)+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5+b \\\\ b & =22.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+22.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=20$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+22.5 \\\\  & =0.5(20)+22.5 \\\\  & =10+22.5 \\\\  & =32.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=20$ we have $y≈32.5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c57330b-8507-5a3c-9806-0e8c205fd13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549337} {:answer/id #uuid "1803bbd6-5355-5d9a-9fb7-f4cf0aaa465d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549364} {:answer/id #uuid "a236d02f-5776-5a91-b192-27c106d17a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549623} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eb3f93-e0e4-5ab6-ab1b-9485d4573bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549709} {:answer/id #uuid "ec03f0fb-5fe8-5b76-ae17-c5f458c1c65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3eb3f93-e0e4-5ab6-ab1b-9485d4573bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549709} :answer-field/id #uuid "588b06de-dfd6-5c5b-94a0-cddc954cebc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0d9aadf-c429-54b7-bed3-89ab52703eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-133942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9aa9f94a8de990bdb8332f2efd5c8e945c9bec4847855eebc3fe7f6a3a02a175.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $y$ when $x=23.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(11,8)$ and $(15,7).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/b9/b9dbed9bab9d769f7408006854bd0c159cb980bfa5f4441fe1b4e5753dfbc078.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(11,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(15,7).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-8}{15-11} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =-0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.25$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(11,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+b \\\\ 8 & =-0.25(11)+b \\\\ 8 & =-2.75+b \\\\ b & =10.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+10.75.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=23$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+10.75 \\\\  & =-0.25(23)+10.75 \\\\  & =-5.75+10.75 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=23$ we have $y≈5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=23$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35b193b3-2679-5e57-8519-13a75ee39c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266607} {:answer/id #uuid "63960119-96c8-5a11-ac8b-ce536b8c33fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267020} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5351fc-ea93-5077-808a-2e970121ae5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267106} {:answer/id #uuid "741f39ef-7c89-590a-98d9-9e6e5d60231a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267164} {:answer/id #uuid "e30490b9-cb52-56b4-adaf-fdd3ed2b6536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e30490b9-cb52-56b4-adaf-fdd3ed2b6536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268150} :answer-field/id #uuid "454866ab-76dc-5d3a-ae98-8d88fb102885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4e2e69a-6742-55aa-baed-902f71aaf13b" :question/math-academy-id "q-133943" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7ba7aa1cf49bb5a2dc430d9a21baaa8491aa61d8898f2aad142406c5c569b51b.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $x$ when $y=7.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,4).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/59/59efe1b79b0d032179a5210ee694b0d59ad082853ca9fb80a9d79d9f6444f2bf.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.75$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.75x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75(1)+b \\\\ 1 & =0.75+b \\\\ b & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.75x+0.25.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=7$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 7 & =0.75x+0.25 \\\\ 6.75 & =0.75x \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=7$ we have $x≈9.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192ae570-5a46-5866-b9fd-ad43da2ee1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266349} {:answer/id #uuid "3dde7667-c13a-51fa-ab35-cd70bef85845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266693} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ab67fa-e98a-56ca-a88f-5053693a9d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.5$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267598} {:answer/id #uuid "d53d2b25-b334-5e73-8347-c08bfb665cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268043} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0f9b62-4ade-57eb-befe-288cb3eb585f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dde7667-c13a-51fa-ab35-cd70bef85845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ pounds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266693} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbfb3a7-3342-516e-8228-b0c951af2f3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81a0f2ef-8e06-5acd-bc49-13e95e8478d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133961" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3d0f871d60b7f7c8280abd7d3eba4337f910bae6b814586b368bbfb0a8dc313.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the weight loss per week (in pounds) vs. the number of hours spent training per week for a group of people. Use the trend line to estimate how much weight a person loses if they train for $4$ hours per week." :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $x=4$ on the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/1e/1e397473de815e2eda88ad33c59e2cf77c336b968ef6a1eeaffa67d9fbc64750.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/27/27c8ce8a7a1feadd4ea16d66f6a283fa245fa737036810924ead4b0148c6d6f5.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/a1/a178070d1f62538a35e7e4e363a254c2afa776920079618b80b3bff358601871.png)\n\nTherefore, a person training $4$ hours a week will lose around $3$ pounds of weight."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41db18df-a534-5726-b39d-8b9562beddc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.25$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549453} {:answer/id #uuid "7378e9b9-c866-5489-8c98-278113aa0897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549537} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1ecf06-594b-58b4-b365-6708b2e5e0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.75$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549638} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e37f8a-7f29-5bb4-a7b5-9b3b74038eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549785} {:answer/id #uuid "fa18fd73-7cda-5618-806a-041e5424f8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8e37f8a-7f29-5bb4-a7b5-9b3b74038eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549785} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a3ec9d-c696-5081-a8d8-ff61a574a27d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73f7b139-c8da-5c53-9a35-10e77ca9a819" :question/math-academy-id "q-133962" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f38e64442bb383f218dede5a88740653f958864d3284ce09c3e1bb99ed7e421.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the time (in hours) some college students spend studying the night before an exam and the time they spend sleeping that night. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time a student spends studying if that student gets only $3.75$ hours of sleep.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,7.5)$ and $(4,5).$*" :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/9e/9e1653b2acbe5c5924e2d562308b2640c64f317ed7500fba4e149c926c9205ab.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,7.5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,5).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-7.5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2.5)}{2} \\\\  & =-1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1.25$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-1.25x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.25x+b \\\\ 5 & =-1.25(4)+b \\\\ 5 & =-5+b \\\\ b & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-1.25x+10.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=3.75$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.25x+10 \\\\ 3.75 & =-1.25x+10 \\\\ 1.25x & =6.25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the student spends approximately $5$ hours studying.\n\nNote: The point where $y=3.75$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3219263b-abe6-5728-9661-5138a2700247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266585} {:answer/id #uuid "381bfb6f-c43f-59ee-a686-ab558a709a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266631} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc2d41a-ea63-5ca1-9147-b914f0535d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266957} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0c2701-9911-51b4-900b-6cab03bd2c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267091} {:answer/id #uuid "eec77403-0e51-5d0c-9b05-535493cd6148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3219263b-abe6-5728-9661-5138a2700247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266585} :answer-field/id #uuid "8409954c-10b2-59ea-b026-9a4d57399061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6bab4ea-9515-5432-8897-bfe0c69aa515" :question/math-academy-id "q-133967" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d4df2d201ccbaf2305534d61c8a0ed5f7957b1629d8cd2fbd5fff933283c0dc.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the number of matches won by some chess players in a championship and the hours they spend training per day.\nUse the trend line to estimate how much time players need to train each day to win $30$ championship games." :question/worked-solution "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=30$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/26/26ba10258a8794a97dfc5e61c8160b342ed6890f7a9af40c1e21ac814c779188.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/e5/e50d79adb9803f6509314cf3512fcd388ccc78b287d6653b9e318ce54ce45022.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/a9/a9bd5a7973ecb2e88c328d0943d4bfebe9862b2cea1abbd0b3fa3665fa177167.png)\n\nTherefore, a chess player that wins $30$ games spends around $5$ hours training per day."}]}
